00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.560 align:start position:0%
 So the next piece of bringing AI everywhere

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:49.640 align:start position:0%
 is edge and enterprise.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.280 align:start position:0%
 And we see that, you know, the edge

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:54.280 align:start position:0%
 is this conglomeration of lots of different platforms,

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.120 align:start position:0%
 lots of different solutions.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:01.160 align:start position:0%
 But the killer app for the next generation of edges, AI,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.800 align:start position:0%
 right, and all of sudden video analytics,

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.800 align:start position:0%
 taking advantage of these vast quantities of data

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.960 align:start position:0%
 that at the edge and largely wasted today.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:15.200 align:start position:0%
 And Intel's been delivering edge platforms for many decades.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.880 align:start position:0%
 And now we're building on those open standard platform

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:23.280 align:start position:0%
 investments, enabling you to bring these new analytical

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:28.120 align:start position:0%
 processes, data, you know, harvesting to those platforms.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.960 align:start position:0%
 And I call it the three laws of the edge.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.600 align:start position:0%
 And we see this idea of AI edge becoming really important.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.720 align:start position:0%
 But should the AI happen in the cloud?

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.680 align:start position:0%
 We're at the edge.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.960 align:start position:0%
 And I call it the three laws, the laws of economics,

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.680 align:start position:0%
 the laws of physics, and the laws of the land.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.280 align:start position:0%
 You know, economics is simply too expensive

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:50.840 align:start position:0%
 to bring your data back to the cloud,

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:52.520 align:start position:0%
 it gives you better cost control.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.360 align:start position:0%
 You know, bandwidth is the most expensive resource,

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:56.520 align:start position:0%
 you know, that you have.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.560 align:start position:0%
 And cloud is the most expensive compute

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.160 align:start position:0%
 that you have in the fleet.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:05.240 align:start position:0%
 So the laws of economics, move it to the edge.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:11.440 align:start position:0%
 But second is the laws of the physics, as I like to say.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:16.360 align:start position:0%
 And how many of you have changed the speed of like this week?

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.960 align:start position:0%
 You know, hey, you know, whether it's skill shortages,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:22.480 align:start position:0%
 quality, security, all of those things, you know,

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.160 align:start position:0%
 what's the round trip requirement?

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.200 align:start position:0%
 What's the user interface expectations

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.360 align:start position:0%
 for instantaneous response?

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.920 align:start position:0%
 Or how fast does the robotic arm need a response?

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.060 align:start position:0%
 If it's 20 milliseconds, you can't 200 millisecond round

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:36.320 align:start position:0%
 trip to the cloud.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.440 align:start position:0%
 The laws of physics draw you to the edge.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.840 align:start position:0%
 And you know, hey, we're pretty profound technologists,

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.120 align:start position:0%
 but we ain't changing the speed of light.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:50.200 align:start position:0%
 But maybe the third is the most powerful, the laws of the land.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:53.280 align:start position:0%
 And we often need to keep data on prem for privacy,

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.440 align:start position:0%
 regulatory security reasons.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.720 align:start position:0%
 And data laws have become increasingly

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:01.400 align:start position:0%
 diverse and restrictive by regions across the world.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:05.240 align:start position:0%
 And you know, every nation has some form of GDPR, you know,

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.480 align:start position:0%
 capabilities in this volume of data

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.600 align:start position:0%
 to tap in at the edges, simply staggering.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.120 align:start position:0%
 You know, and again, I was with the CIO recently.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:15.600 align:start position:0%
 And he was describing the 100 plus companies

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.960 align:start position:0%
 they operate in at all the different data privacy

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.600 align:start position:0%
 and regulatory requirements.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:23.680 align:start position:0%
 The idea of bringing data back to the cloud?

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.640 align:start position:0%
 No way was his response.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:30.160 align:start position:0%
 Couldn't even fathom the idea of moving data out of region

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.040 align:start position:0%
 for these use cases.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.760 align:start position:0%
 So the three laws.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:36.520 align:start position:0%
 And as the edge becomes increasingly important,

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.960 align:start position:0%
 it's going to become, we believe, the dominant AI workload

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:45.520 align:start position:0%
 research indicates that by 2026, 50% of edge computing

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.880 align:start position:0%
 deployments will involve machine learning and AI

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:54.160 align:start position:0%
 compared to just 5% today, a killer use case.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.360 align:start position:0%
 Now, one of the things I've really become infatuated with

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:00.440 align:start position:0%
 is we've studied these AI use cases.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:04.200 align:start position:0%
 It's all about unleashing your data.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.280 align:start position:0%
 And how does your, you know, this massive proprietary data

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:13.280 align:start position:0%
 sets, how can we turn it loose, you know, for AI deployments?

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:17.840 align:start position:0%
 And Rags, retrieval augmented generation, is like so cool.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:23.360 align:start position:0%
 It's sort of this perfect bridge between cool new LLMs

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:27.280 align:start position:0%
 and the big data repositories that you have today.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:31.360 align:start position:0%
 And Rags becomes, I think, one of the most important workflows

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.840 align:start position:0%
 for corporations to take advantage of their data

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:39.680 align:start position:0%
 and generative AI, where I can take modern open source LLMs.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.400 align:start position:0%
 And, you know, maybe if you're really good,

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.520 align:start position:0%
 you're updating and retraining those.

